WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.790
Abraham: When changes are made to
a system or network there is the

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:05.910
potential to create new vulnerabilities.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:09.610
It is therefore imperative that
further testing and retesting

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:11.009
is done to mitigate this.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.900
Nicole: This may be done on
a regular basis and is called

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:16.320
remediation verification testing.

00:00:17.299 --> 00:00:21.580
Remediation verification testing
makes sure that any vulnerabilities

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.240
identified during pen testing
have been dealt with successfully.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.750
It focusses on ensuring that issues that
arose have been properly identified,

00:00:30.799 --> 00:00:32.709
fixed and are no longer a threat.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:36.580
Abraham: It works by attempting
to reproduce each vulnerability

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.360
and then checking if it is fixed.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.080
Insurers and regulators often
require this to be done by an

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.439
independent authority to make sure
the testing and solution is robust.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:51.750
Nicole: A penetration test can only
however, validate that an organisations

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:56.140
IT systems are not vulnerable to any
known issues as of the day of the test.

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:01.270
Unless tests are done on a regular basis,
vulnerability can last in a system for

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.279
some time before it is checked again.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.700
How are the outcomes of cyber
security testing reported?

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:12.690
Abraham: Once testing is done the most
common form of reporting is the detailed

00:01:12.699 --> 00:01:14.800
reports usually sent to senior management.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.070
Smaller tests and regular scans
may be reported as memos, incident

00:01:20.099 --> 00:01:22.100
logs or as a short report.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.899
Reports are responsible for detailing all
the tasks performed by a security tester

00:01:28.199 --> 00:01:32.950
or ethical hacker and are responsible
for detailing key findings and steps

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.119
needed to act on these by remediation.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:41.049
Nicole: Reports may be viewed by senior
management, IT teams, clients or even

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.640
the regulators and should be clear and
concise whilst setting out any details

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.620
of the problems and the solutions.

00:01:48.699 --> 00:01:52.289
Abraham: A good report should also
include a summary or overview of any

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.600
findings and recommendations as well
as a comprehensive detailed list

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.280
of issues and proposed solutions.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.490
This then should make it clear to
all levels of interested parties.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:05.489
Nicole: A test report should include:

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.039
• any security issues that are uncovered,

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:13.170
• an assessment of the level of risk that
each vulnerability exposes the system to.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.899
• a method or resolving each issue.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:20.620
• An opinion on the accuracy of the
vulnerability assessment and advice

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.420
on how to improve the process.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:27.750
The process of debriefing is also very
important, and vulnerabilities can be

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:32.130
discussed according to their severity
and if a test shows vulnerabilities

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.060
then solutions can be found.

00:02:34.599 --> 00:02:38.940
Abraham: So, why should the outcomes
of cyber security testing be reported?

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:42.780
There are many reasons for reporting
the outcomes of cyber security

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:46.650
testing but whatever the reason
these results should only be

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.150
shared with those who need to know.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.270
They can include sensitive highly
confidential information which should

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.630
only be made available to trusted parties.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:59.559
Nicole: However, a summary version
of a report can be more widely shared

00:02:59.809 --> 00:03:03.940
detailing the scope and approach of an
attack and how this can be mitigated.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:08.460
Abraham: Websites, organisations
and software developers often

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.510
offer a ‘bug bounty’ to uncover
issues and vulnerabilities that may

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:14.649
impact their product or systems.

00:03:15.309 --> 00:03:18.660
For those who find complex security
vulnerabilities the payout can be

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.690
high but on average payouts are low.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.610
Nicole: There are also companies that
offer the service of connecting ‘bug

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:29.850
bounty hunters’ to companies to help
with finding their vulnerability issues.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:34.570
However, for some organisations, there
may be other reasons for reporting the

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:36.420
outcomes of cyber security testing.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:41.130
One of these may be regulatory to
demonstrate to the appropriate authorities

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:45.799
that they are taking steps to identify
and eliminate any vulnerabilities.

00:03:46.389 --> 00:03:50.699
They may also be required by
regulatory bodies to conduct regular

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.830
testing especially after a breach.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:58.110
Abraham: A key principle of GDPR
regulations is that personal data

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:02.350
is processed securely, and all
systems and services have the

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.150
correct security to maintain this.

00:04:04.719 --> 00:04:09.829
The National Cyber Security Centre
[NCSC] even offers a service to

00:04:09.829 --> 00:04:12.999
organisations to report cyber
security incidents simplifying

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.150
the reporting landscape for users.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.640
Nicole: It is therefore especially
important that if an organisation has

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:23.900
had a data breach it shows that it
is taking all the appropriate steps

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.510
to stop this from happening again.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:29.280
Abraham: Reporting testing results
is the best way that an organisation

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.350
can regain trust after a breach
and reassure their clients and

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.350
customers that their system is secure.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.060
Nicole: This can also help an organization
to better plan for any future threats

00:04:40.349 --> 00:04:43.799
and allocate the appropriate resources
to help prevent this happening.

